(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-lab-diagnostics-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics Quiz 2 — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which activity belongs to the intratest phase?","choices":["Monitoring vital signs and administering analgesics or sedatives during the procedure","Identifying contraindications to the test","Ordering appropriate follow-up laboratory studies","Assessing the patient's coping styles, fears and phobias before the procedure is scheduled"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the intratest phase covers performing the test and everything it encompasses. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What distinguishes a screening test from a diagnostic test?","choices":["Screening is performed only after a diagnostic test has returned an abnormal result that requires further explanation","Screening looks for evidence of disease in an asymptomatic person; a diagnostic test looks for the reason for symptoms","Screening is always more invasive than diagnostic testing","Screening provides the definitive diagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the presence or absence of symptoms is the discriminator. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which is described as the highest-volume point-of-care test using a single-use quantitative cartridge or strip with a reader?","choices":["Rapid strep","Urinalysis dipstick","Urine pregnancy testing","Glucose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — listed alongside blood chemistries, coagulation studies, cardiac markers and hemoglobin A1c. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A patient asks why you are ordering a laboratory test when you already suspect the diagnosis. Which statement best describes the role of laboratory testing?","choices":["It is a tool used alongside a thorough history and physical examination to add information","It replaces the history and physical examination once the results have been reported","It is primarily therapeutic rather than diagnostic","It is ordered chiefly to satisfy documentation requirements"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diagnostics supplement the history and examination rather than replacing them. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which of the following is an indication for stool studies?","choices":["Chronic cough with sputum production","Fever with a suspicion of bloodstream infection or septicemia","Recent travel, consumption of well water, or prolonged antibiotic use","Unexplained weight gain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside diarrhea, excessive flatus, abdominal discomfort and change in stool color. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which statement best summarises the relationship between a test and the population it is used in?","choices":["Neither is affected by where the test is performed","Sensitivity and specificity belong to the test; predictive value belongs to the population being tested","Both the test characteristics and the predictive values belong to the test and travel with it between populations","Both belong to the population and vary from place to place"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the test stays the same, and prevalence changes the meaning of the result. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which testing complexity category accounts for roughly 75% of the twelve thousand available laboratory tests?","choices":["Provider-performed microscopy","Moderately complex testing","Waived testing","Highly complex testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — usually automated, and the majority of tests in use. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which posttest activities are grouped under integration and follow-up?","choices":["Monitoring vital signs and administering sedation","Reviewing history and identifying contraindications","Selecting the appropriate collection tube, observing the correct order of draw, and labeling each specimen","Ordering appropriate follow-up laboratory studies, scheduling follow-up, and making appropriate referrals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside patient education and consideration of emotional well-being. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"After the puncture site has been disinfected for a blood culture, what must be avoided?","choices":["Palpating the site, unless sterile gloves are worn","Allowing the antiseptic to dry before puncture","Drawing the aerobic bottle first","Using the opposite arm for the second sample"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — palpating after disinfection recontaminates the site. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does positive predictive value indicate?","choices":["The proportion of the population that has the disease","The likelihood that the test will be negative when disease is absent","The likelihood that a positive test result identifies someone with the disease","The likelihood that the test will be positive when disease is present"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and negative predictive value is the likelihood that a negative result identifies someone without it. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What must a stool specimen be free of?","choices":["Dietary fibre residue","Water from the toilet bowl, which dilutes the specimen","Urine or other bodily secretions such as menstrual blood","Any trace of normal gut flora"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it must be collected into a dry, clean container. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How is point-of-care testing defined?","choices":["Medical testing completed outside the centralized laboratory, at or close to the site of patient care","Any laboratory test with a turnaround time under one hour","Testing performed by a licensed clinician rather than by a laboratory technologist or technician","Testing that does not require a laboratory certificate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — also called near-patient, remote, satellite laboratory testing or rapid diagnostics. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which elements should patient education about a diagnostic test include?","choices":["Only the preparation instructions, since everything else is better discussed once results return","Only the cost, since that is what most affects the decision","Only the risks, since consent is the legal requirement","The testing process, answers to their questions, and the anticipated timeline for results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all three are named as part of pretest patient and family education. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which agency issues laboratory certificates and conducts inspections?","choices":["The Food and Drug Administration","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","The National Institutes of Health","The Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it also collects user fees, approves accreditation organizations and publishes the rules. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Specificity relates to fewer of which kind of error?","choices":["False positives","False negatives","Both false positives and false negatives equally","Neither; specificity addresses precision rather than error type"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and specificity does not address false negatives. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which two areas of point-of-care testing are noted as rapidly increasing?","choices":["Rapid strep testing and rapid influenza antigen testing","Fentanyl testing and human immunodeficiency virus testing","Cholesterol and hemoglobin A1c testing","Pregnancy and ovulation testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both are flagged as growing rapidly. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"When does the posttest phase begin?","choices":["Once the test is complete, focusing on patient aftercare","Once the result has been communicated to the patient","Once the specimen has been transported to the laboratory","Once follow-up testing has been ordered"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — aftercare, interpretation, communication and follow-up. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How often must a testing site reapply for its certificate?","choices":["Every five years","Only when the scope of testing changes","Every two years","Every year"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the certificate must match the level of testing complexity performed. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"You are ordering coagulation studies. Which tube should the specimen arrive in?","choices":["Light blue","Lavender","Gray","Green"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — light blue contains sodium citrate and is the coagulation tube. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which category describes slide examination of a freshly collected specimen by a provider, such as a Gram stain or manual cell count?","choices":["Moderately complex testing","Highly complex testing","Provider-performed microscopy","Waived testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a distinct complexity category. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What does the integration component of the posttest phase refer to?","choices":["Reconciling the result against the pretest probability","Diagnosis, subsequent acceptance, healing, and health-promoting behavior","Combining results from several laboratories into a single unified record","Entering results into the electronic health record"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it includes patient education for the diagnosis and treatment plan. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which principle is stated about documentation in the posttest phase?","choices":["Verbal handoff is sufficient when the result is communicated directly","“If it wasn't documented, it wasn't done”","Documentation may be deferred until results are final","Only abnormal results require documentation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — detailed documentation is described as essential to accurate record keeping and reporting. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which is listed as an advantage of point-of-care testing?","choices":["Greater precision than the equivalent central laboratory method in every case","Lower cost than central laboratory testing","Standardised vocabulary across manufacturers","Better care where resources are limited, such as rural settings or disaster zones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside convenience, speed, less manpower, fewer visits and fingerstick rather than needle stick. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which factors should be considered when communicating about diagnostic testing?","choices":["Only the patient's stated educational level","Only whether an interpreter is required","Only the urgency of the clinical situation","Ethnicity, culture, gender and age"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — these are named alongside preparing the patient properly and following standards. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A state enacts laboratory regulation that differs from the federal Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments. What is the relationship?","choices":["Federal regulation applies only where no state rule exists","State regulation applies only to hospital laboratories","State and city regulation will always be stricter, because the federal standard is the minimum and cannot be downgraded","State regulation supersedes the federal standard in either direction, so a state may relax the requirement if it chooses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — states may add requirements but may never relax them. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does incorporating prevalence into sensitivity produce?","choices":["The pre-test probability applying to the next patient who is tested","The clinically useful positive predictive value, by way of Bayes' theorem","The specificity of the test in that population","The incidence of the condition"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — moving from a test-centered probability to a patient-centered one. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which quality measure addresses post-testing errors in point-of-care testing?","choices":["Restricting testing to daylight hours","Connecting devices to electronic information systems","Requiring two operators to witness each result","Repeating every abnormal result in the central laboratory"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — communication breakdown is the named risk, and connectivity is the stated mitigation. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why are three separate random stool specimens recommended for ova and parasite testing?","choices":["Because each specimen tests for a different class of organism","Because the first specimen is always discarded as contaminated","Because the laboratory requires a minimum total volume spread across three containers","Because of the life cycle of parasites, which increases the likelihood of detection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — organisms are shed intermittently, so a single specimen can miss them. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What may be used to help a patient who cannot produce a sputum specimen?","choices":["Aerosols such as sterile water, saline or albuterol","Chest percussion alone, without any aerosol","A prolonged breath hold before coughing","Delaying collection until the following morning"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — these help loosen and induce secretions. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How should the swab be applied when obtaining a throat culture?","choices":["Swab a single tonsil to minimise the gag reflex","Swab the soft palate and uvula only, deliberately avoiding the tonsils so that the gag reflex is not triggered","Rotate it firmly and gently over the posterior throat, both tonsils, and any areas of inflammation, exudation or ulceration","Swab the anterior tongue and the inner cheeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the tongue and lips must not be touched. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 26"}]};
